PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Round-0 answers cluster around four values (32, 22, 14, 10) driven by two axes of disagreement: (1) whether the single-orientation constraint permits only one transition direction (A→B) or both, and (2) whether two obligatory crossovers in the pedigree enforce even transition parity (0 or 2) or allow 0/1/2.  
Strongest arguments: Colleague 2’s orientation restriction yields only 4+6=10 reachable strings; Colleagues 10/11 argue even parity after exactly two meioses → 2+12=14; multiple colleagues (3,4,6,8) enumerate all strings with ≤2 transitions → 2+8+12=22, treating both starting alleles and both relative placements of the second crossover as admissible. 32 responses ignore the two-meiosis limit.  
I currently lean toward 14 (parity from two fixed-orientation events) or 10 (if orientation truly forbids one starting allele), but the exact interaction between “one orientation” and choice of which homolog participates in the F2 meiosis remains unresolved. Need to simulate the four-interval model explicitly next round.}

