PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Round-1 responses sharpen the orientation axis: unidirectional constraint (“only one orientation” + positive-strand asymmetry) restricts every meiosis to A-to-B switches only, so F1 gametes yield exactly the four A-starting single-transition strings. Second (F2) meiosis between any pair of those strings can add at most one more allowed transition, producing precisely the same four single-transition + six double-transition strings (C(4,2) placements). No zero-transition parentals are recoverable (unidirectional crossovers cannot cancel) and no three-plus transitions are possible after two meioses, yielding 10.  
Arguments for 14/22/32 either allow both directions or treat the two meioses as parity filters without the one-orientation rule; both appear inconsistent with the problem wording.  
Four-interval enumeration remains the core model; need to confirm whether any F2 genotype can still produce an A-starting single-transition in F3 or whether all patterns are already covered by the 10. Current lean: 10.}

